 "credits": {
  "words": "Theodore Baker (1894), from the Dutch \"Wilt heden nu treden\" in Adrianus Valerius's Nederlandtsche Gedenck-clanck (1626)",
  "tune": "\"Kremser\", a Netherlands folk tune printed by Valerius (1626), as Eduard Kremser arranged it",
  "arrangement": "The four parts for the tune Kremser as The Beacon Hymnal printed them (1924), adapted by Stackapella"
 },
 "licence": {
  "words": "public domain: Baker's English published 1894 (Baker died 1934); the Dutch printed 1626 (Valerius died 1625)",
  "tune": "public domain: printed 1626 (Valerius died 1625); Kremser's arrangement printed by 1924 (Kremser died 1914)",
  "arrangement": "Eduard Kremser's four parts for Kremser as The Beacon Hymnal printed them (public domain: published 1924; its editor, Florence Buck, died 1925; the same parts printed as \"Arr. by E. Kremser\", Kremser died 1914), in its key of D: its tune and bass as printed, except the bass's A on \"his\" (bar 6) sung an octave lower so it does not leap a seventh to the B after it, and the bass notes named below; the alto and tenor revoiced in places so the duet sings a third or sixth on the strong beats: \"We gath-er to-\" (alto F sharp and G over the tune's A and B, tenor A), \"will to make known\" (alto D, D, D, C sharp, tenor F sharp, F sharp, G sharp, A), \"op-press-ing now cease\" (alto F sharp), \"from dis-\" (alto E, tenor C sharp), \"Sing prais-es\" (alto A then B, tenor F sharp then D), \"chas-tens and\" (alto C sharp, D, E; bass A, C sharp, E), \"wick-ed\" (alto C sharp, D; tenor A), \"name,\" and \"side,\" (alto C sharp, bass A), \"he for-gets\" and \"all glo-\" (tenor F sharp, E); the tenor's \"the\" in the first line on G, not B, so it does not move in fifths with the tune; in \"has-tens\" the alto, tenor and bass change chord with the tune's E on the half beat, not on the beat, and under the tune's slurred notes (\"name\", \"side\", \"fight\") the tenor holds its first note, so every harmony part sings one note to a syllable; the last chord of each verse complete, with the alto a third above the tune's low D and the tenor on A; the Amen left out; breaths written in for beginners. Accepted exceptions (singable.py): the alto a third above the tune's lowest note, as the licence says: where the tune ends each verse on its low D (\"own.\" and \"thine!\"), the alto sings F sharp above it, so the duet ends on the chord's third, and with the tenor's A and the bass's D the last chord is complete.",
  "recordings": "synthesized guides",
  "checked": null,
  "source": "The Beacon Hymnal, for Church Schools, Young People's Services, Day Schools, and the Home, edited by Florence Buck (Boston: The Beacon Press, 1924), No. 17, \"We gather together\", headed \"Kremser. 12, 11, 12, 11\", \"Anon.\" and \"Old Netherlands Melody, 1625\", \"Sing in unison\": its four parts in D, in 3/4 with a one-beat pickup, and verses 1 and 2 as printed under the music; the copy of Princeton Theological Seminary Library, archive.org/details/beaconhymnalforc00unse/page/n147 (Buck, 1860 to 1925: archive.org/details/storyofjesusmanu00buck). The same parts are printed as \"Arr. by E. Kremser\" in The Abingdon Hymnal (1928), No. 207 (archive.org/details/abingdonhymnal0000earl/page/n257). Breaths: the last note of the first and third lines (\"ing,\") shortened to half a beat and the next line's pickup (\"He\", \"Sing\", \"Or-\", \"Thou,\") started a quarter beat late; \"known;\" and \"vine,\" shortened to a beat and a quarter; verse 1's \"own.\" shortened to a beat and a quarter before verse 2's pickup; in every part. Baker's English also read in The American Student Hymnal, edited by H. Augustine Smith (New York: Fleming H. Revell, 1928), p. 27, No. 29, \"Kremser. No. 27\", as words only, signed \"Translated by Dr. Theodore Baker\" with \"English version copyright, 1894, by G. Schirmer\" (archive.org/details/americanstudenth00warm/page/n64); its verses 1 and 2 are word for word The Beacon Hymnal's. The Dutch printed as \"Wilt heden nu treden voor God den Heere\" in Adrianus Valerius, Nederlandtsche Gedenck-clanck (1626), p. 170, to the tune \"Hey wilder dan wild\" (archive.org/details/bub_gb_wxxKAAAAcAAJ/page/n183)."
 },
